        'en' => '<h2>Non-Surgical Laser Treatment for Lumbar Disc Herniation</h2> <p><strong>Non-surgical laser treatment for lumbar disc herniation</strong> is one of the modern methods used to relieve the pain caused by a lumbar disc herniation. With this technique, doctors treat the herniated disc directly. This reduces the pressure on the nerve, and patients feel relief within a short time. In addition, the procedure requires no surgical incision, and patients can return to their daily lives quickly.</p> <h3>What Is Non-Surgical Laser Treatment for Lumbar Disc Herniation?</h3> <p>In this treatment method, the doctor uses laser energy to reduce the pressure inside the disc. When the disc pressure falls, the herniated part moves away from the nerve and the pain eases. In the medical literature this technique is called <strong>PLDD (Percutaneous Laser Disc Decompression)</strong>.</p> <p>During the procedure, the specialist doctor uses an imaging device called a fluoroscope. This allows the laser fibre to be directed exactly to the point where the herniation is located, and the treatment is completed without damaging the surrounding tissue.</p> <h3>Advantages of Non-Surgical Laser Treatment for Lumbar Disc Herniation</h3> <p>Compared with conventional lumbar disc herniation surgery, this method offers patients a much more comfortable process.</p> <ul>
<li><strong>No incision or stitches are needed.</strong> The doctor uses only a fine needle.</li>
<li><strong>No general anaesthesia is used.</strong> Specialists perform the procedure under local anaesthesia.</li>
<li><strong>The recovery time is short.</strong> Patients are usually discharged on foot within a few hours.</li>
<li><strong>The risk of complications is low.</strong> The likelihood of bleeding and infection is very small.</li>
</ul> <h3>How Is Non-Surgical Laser Treatment for Lumbar Disc Herniation Performed?</h3> <p>During the procedure, the specialist doctor uses an X-ray imaging system and determines the location of the herniation clearly. Then, with the help of a fine needle, the inside of the disc is reached. Next, the laser fibre is advanced through the needle and delivers controlled energy.</p> <p>The laser energy vaporises part of the fluid inside the disc. This reduces the pressure in the disc and the herniated tissue is drawn back inwards. Once the pressure on the nerve is relieved, the patient\'s pain decreases quickly. The whole procedure is usually completed within 15-20 minutes.</p> <h3>Comparison of Non-Surgical Laser Treatment for Lumbar Disc Herniation and Surgery</h3> <table>
